     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**Yes, it is a strong baseline offer, but you should not accept it as-is.** \n\nBecause Google recruited you while you are happy and well-compensated at Meta, you hold significant leverage. \n\n---\n\n### 2. Year-by-Year Compensation Breakdown\n\nGoogle typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial equity grants: **38% in Year 1, 32% in Year 2, 20% in Year 3, and 10% in Year 4**. \n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 | Google Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) |\n| **Initial RSUs/GSUs** | ~$250,000 | $410,400 (38%) | $345,600 (32%) | $216,000 (20%) | $108,000 (10%) |\n| **Expected Total** | **~$526,000** | **~$692,400** | **~$627,600\\*** | **~$498,000\\*** | **~$390,000\\*** |\n\n*\\*Excludes future annual refresher grants, which Google typically vests over 4 years (25%/year).*\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **Year 1 & 2 Upside:** Due to front-loading, your Year 1 cash flow will jump from **$526k to ~$692k** (a **+31% increase**), and Year 2 remains well above your Meta comp.\n2. **The Year 3\u20134 Equity Cliff:** In Years 3 and 4, the initial grant tapers off sharply. Refresher grants are designed to backfill this drop, but as the recruiter noted, refreshers depend on performance ratings and company allocation.\n3. **The Base Salary Cut:** Google is offering **$235k**, which is a **$5,000 pay cut** relative to your current Meta base.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weak Spots You Can Leverage\n\n1. **Base Salary is Below Meta:** You should not take a base pay reduction. The posted salary band reaches up to **$271,000**, meaning there is room to move upward.\n2. **Missing Sign-On Bonus ($0):** Google frequently excludes sign-on bonuses from opening offers, holding them as a primary lever during negotiation. For L6 roles, sign-on bonuses typically range from **$50,000 to $100,000+**.\n3. **Unvested Meta RSUs:** Walking away from Meta means leaving behind unvested equity and upcoming performance refreshers.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nDo not reject the offer or walk away outright; **negotiate on three specific components:**\n\n| Component | Initial Offer | Counter-Offer Target | Realistic Settlement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$260,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $255,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$100,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $75,000 |\n| **4-Year Equity** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000** | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n\n* **Total Year 1 with Target Counter:** ~$830,000\n* **Total Year 1 with Realistic Settlement:** ~$750,000 \u2013 $780,000\n\n---\n\n### 5. How to Frame the Negotiation (Script)\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending this offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of the role. Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and upcoming vests at Meta, making a move needs to be compelling across all components of compensation.*\n>\n> *A few key points on the numbers:*\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** The current base of $235k is below my current $240k base at Meta. Given the posted range up to $271k, I\u2019d like to bring the base to **$260,000**.\n> 2. * **Sign-On Bonus:** Leaving Meta means walking away from upcoming vests and performance grants. To bridge that transition, I\u2019m requesting a **$100,000 sign-on bonus**.\n> 3. * **Equity:** Looking at the 38/32/20/10 vesting structure and the out-year step down, an equity grant of **$1.25M** would ensure strong alignment with long-term retention.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to these targets, I am ready to sign and begin planning my start date.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist\n* **Do not accept immediately:** Opening offers rarely represent Google's ceiling.\n* **Do not walk away:** They want you, and you have strong leverage.\n* **Anchor your counter-offer** on the base salary cut, unvested Meta equity, and the Year 3\u20134 vesting step-down. Push for a **$250k+ base**, a **$50k\u2013$100k sign-on bonus**, and **$1.15M\u2013$1.25M equity**.",
