     "content": "### The Verdict: **Yes, take it (if you want the role/company change).**\n\nThis is an **exceptional, top-of-market offer for an L6 Product Manager at Google**. \n\nWhile they held firm on the sign-on bonus, they compensated by boosting your 4-year equity grant to **$1.28M** (an increase of $200,000). Because Google uses a front-loaded vesting schedule (38/32/20/10), that equity increase acts like a massive rolling sign-on bonus over your first two years.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Updated Year-by-Year Breakdown\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 | Google Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) | $48,000 (20%) |\n| **RSU Vesting** | ~$250,000 | **$486,400** (38%) | **$409,600** (32%) | **$256,000** (20%) | **$128,000** (10%) |\n| **Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$774,400** | **~$697,600\\*** | **~$544,000\\*** | **~$416,000\\*** |\n\n*\\*Excludes future annual refresher grants.*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the Numbers Make Sense\n\n1. **They Fully Covered Your Unvested Meta Equity:**\n   * You mentioned leaving behind ~$200k in unvested Meta RSUs over the next 12 months.\n   * Google's Year 1 equity vest alone is **$486,400**\u2014more than double what was vesting at Meta in the same timeframe.\n2. **Huge 2-Year Wealth Acceleration:**\n   * Over your first **24 months**, Google will pay you **~$1.47M in total compensation**, compared to **~$1.05M** if you stay at Meta at your current run rate.\n   * That is an extra **~$420,000 pre-tax surplus** over 2 years.\n3. **Top Decile for L6 PM:**\n   * A typical Google L6 PM initial equity grant sits between $750k and $1M. A **$1.28M grant** is at the highest band for an L6 hire (often approaching lower L7 territory).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Push Any Further?\n\n**No.** When Google increases an equity grant by $200k to hit $1.28M, matches your base, and explicitly states *\"this is our best offer,\"* they have hit the compensation committee's ceiling for the band without requiring an L7 up-level.\n\nPushing further without a new competing written offer carries real diminishing returns and could test recruiter goodwill.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Non-Financial Factors to Make Your Final Call\n\nSince the financial upside is definitively there, your decision comes down to scope and career goals:\n\n* **Scope & Domain:** Is the product area at Google more exciting, higher impact, or strategic for your resume than your current Meta team?\n* **Pacing & Culture:** Meta is generally higher velocity and more execution-heavy with bottom-up pressure; Google tends to be more consensus-driven, with better work-life balance on average.\n* **Refresher Mechanics:** At Google, perform well in Years 1 and 2 to capture annual refreshers; those will stack to cushion the Year 3 and 4 initial grant drop-off.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Accept the offer.** You successfully pushed them to match your base and extracted an extra $200k in front-loaded equity, giving you a **~$248k pay bump (+47%) in Year 1**.",
